      "text": "To solve for \\(2a + 3b\\) where \\(a = 2 - i\\) and \\(b = -1 + i\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute the given values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) into the expression \\(2a + 3b\\).\n2. Distribute the constants 2 and 3 to each term inside the parentheses.\n3. Combine the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\nLet's start with the substitution:\n\n\\[2a + 3b = 2(2 - i) + 3(-1 + i)\\]\n\nNext, distribute the constants 2 and 3:\n\n\\[2(2 - i) = 2 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-i) = 4 - 2i\\]\n\\[3(-1 + i) = 3 \\cdot (-1) + 3 \\cdot i = -3 + 3i\\]\n\nNow, combine these results:\n\n\\[2a + 3b = (4 - 2i) + (-3 + 3i)\\]\n\nCombine the real parts and the imaginary parts separately:\n\nReal part: \\(4 + (-3) = 1\\)\nImaginary part: \\(-2i + 3i = i\\)\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[2a + 3b = 1 + i\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{1 + i}\\]",
